  "fullText": "**Git branch**\n\n- A *branch* is an internal Git construct that lets you diverge from the main line of development within a single repository.\n- It lives only in the repo (local or remote) where it’s created.\n- All branches share the same history and objects; switching branches is just moving the HEAD pointer.\n- Typical use: develop a feature, fix a bug, or experiment without affecting other work until you merge (or rebase) the branch back.\n\n**GitHub fork**\n\n- A *fork* is a copy of an entire repository that lives under a different GitHub account (or organization).\n- It creates a separate remote repository with its own branches, issues, pull‑requests, etc.\n- Forks are used primarily for collaborating across project boundaries: you can modify the code independently and later propose changes back to the upstream repo via a pull request.\n- Because it’s a distinct repo, you can push, set CI, manage access, and keep the fork up‑to‑date with the upstream independently.\n\n**Key differences**\n\n| Aspect | Branch | Fork |\n|--------|--------|------|\n| Scope | Within a single repository | Separate repository (often under another user/org) |\n| Visibility | Same remote; collaborators see all branches | Independent repo; only its owner (and collaborators) see its branches |\n| Use case | Short‑term work, feature development, CI pipelines | Long‑term or external contributions, open‑source contributions, independent maintenance |\n| Merge flow | Direct merge/rebase within the same repo | Pull request from fork → upstream repo (or vice‑versa) |\n| Permissions | Requires write access to the repo | No write access needed on upstream; you work in your own copy |\n\nIn short, a branch is a lightweight way to diverge *inside* a repo, while a fork is a full copy of the repo that lives elsewhere, enabling independent development and contribution across ownership boundaries.",
